## Theorem `erdos_23.variants.n5_tight`

**Statement.** There exists a triangle-free graph on 25 vertices requiring at least 25 edge removals.

**Proof.** Take $G = \text{blowupC5}(5)$ on $\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z} \times \text{Fin}(5)$, which has $5 \times 5 = 25$ vertices. Transport this to $\text{Fin}(25)$ via the canonical bijection $\text{Fin}(25) \simeq \mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z} \times \text{Fin}(5)$ (since $25 = 5 \cdot 5$); all graph properties are preserved.

- **Triangle-free:** A triangle in $\text{blowupC5}(5)$ would require three vertices $(i,a),(j,b),(k,c)$ with each pair adjacent, meaning each pair of first coordinates is adjacent in $C_5$. But $C_5$ has no triangle, so no such $i,j,k$ exist.
- **At least 25 edges must be removed:** By `blowupC5_tight` with $n=5$, any bipartite $H \le \text{blowupC5}(5)$ satisfies $5^2 = 25 \le |E(\text{blowupC5}(5)) \setminus E(H)|$. The transport to $\text{Fin}(25)$ preserves edge counts. $\square$

---

## Theorems `erdos_23.variants.n5` and `erdos_23` — Status

**`erdos_23.variants.n5`** (research, solved): This theorem states that every triangle-free graph on 25 vertices can be made bipartite by removing at most 25 edges. It is established in the literature via the high-density range result of Balogh–Clemen–Lidický (arXiv:2103.14179) combined with McKay's computational catalogue of 23-vertex extremal graphs for bipartization. A complete proof requires exhaustive computational verification over the extremal graph catalogue and cannot be reproduced as a self-contained mathematical argument here.

**`erdos_23`** (research, open): This is the full Erdős Problem 23 conjecture — whether every triangle-free graph on $5n$ vertices can be made bipartite by deleting at most $n^2$ edges, for all $n$. This remains open. The $C_5$ blow-up construction shows the bound $n^2$ is best possible (as proven in `blowupC5_tight` above), and the cases $n=1$ and $n=5$ are settled, but the general conjecture is unresolved.

